GoldLink's change to melonDS (UC11): the steady overworld keeps the attached objects' pixels hidden
behind the world.

- GPU3D_Soft: where something of the world is in front of an attached object (the player behind
  a windmill's blade, a roof's corner, a person), the attached object's own opaque pixel behind it
  (BehindColor, BehindDepth, the nearest; ShownBehind with the depth): drawn behind it, or covered
  by it later. The player's layer and the world slide by different fractions; the window drew the
  player cut where the world covered it, and what was in front at the world's shift: a gap or an
  overlap all round it (an outline, the user's play test). With these the window draws the
  player whole and what's in front over them.

Applied by build.rs to a copy of the submodule (which stays at upstream).

--- a/src/GPU3D_Soft.h	2026-11-14 13:36:52.133096177 -0500
+++ b/src/GPU3D_Soft.h	2026-11-14 13:36:52.143793706 -0500
@@ -62,6 +62,12 @@
     // and in front of that (UnderDepth). The window shows it where the world slides out from
     // under the player's layer (half a pixel at most), instead of a guess.
     const u32* GetShownUnderLine(int line) const { return &ShownUnder[line * ScanlineWidth]; }
+    // GoldLink (UC11): where something of the world is in front of an attached object, the
+    // attached object's own opaque pixel behind it (ColorBuffer's format; 0: none), the nearest.
+    // The window draws the player's layer whole and what's in front over it with the world's
+    // shift (the two slide apart by a fraction: an outline round anything in front of the player,
+    // the user's play test).
+    const u32* GetShownBehindLine(int line) const { return &ShownBehind[line * ScanlineWidth]; }
 
